<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Nova Scotia</h1><p class="st-meta">1713 CE – 1867 CE · capital Halifax</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British colony formed from French Acadia, scene of the Acadian expulsion, and the first colony in the British Empire to win responsible government.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713 ceded peninsular <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/acadia">Acadia</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, which renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, reviving an earlier Scottish claim. For decades a small garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annapolis-royal">Annapolis Royal</a> ruled a population of French-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a>. In 1749 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-cornwallis">Edward Cornwallis</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halifax-nova-scotia">Halifax</a> as a naval base and new capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Doubting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a>' loyalty, governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-lawrence">Charles Lawrence</a> began their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> in 1755, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> Planters took their farms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia's</a> assembly, first elected in 1758, was the oldest in what is now <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a>. After 1783 tens of thousands of Loyalists arrived, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-loyalists">Black Loyalists</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birchtown">Birchtown</a>, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-new-brunswick">New Brunswick</a> was split off in 1784. Fishing, shipbuilding and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> drove the economy, and in 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-howe">Joseph Howe's</a> reformers won responsible government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1867 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-new-brunswick">New Brunswick</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Province of Canada</a> to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canada">Dominion of Canada</a>, of which it remains a province. Union was unpopular at first, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-howe">Howe</a> led a campaign for repeal before accepting better financial terms in 1869.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-nova-scotia.jpg" alt="Colony of Nova Scotia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Kooma, FJDEACKB, Zscout370 and Denelson83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nova_Scotia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1713 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Halifax</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edward Cornwallis, Charles Lawrence, Joseph Howe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Canada</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nova Scotia</dd></dl></aside></div>
